    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a deck build in vancouver. using western red cedar. can u explain the main difference between live load and dead load for the sizing calcs? need to make sure my post spacing is right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Live loads are the temporary forces that vary over time, such as the weight of people or furniture. Dead loads, on the other hand, are permanent and consistent forces exerted on the deck, like the weight of the decking itself. For sizing calculations, live loads are generally included to ensure the deck can handle temporary occupants or objects, while dead loads are integral as they represent the self-weight of the deck materials. Properly accounting for both will ensure the post spacing is adequate to support the combined effect.",
