    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so for the dead load, i'm thinking of using 2x6 cedar for the decking. i also need to factor in the weight of the heavy railing system we're planning. if i'm trying to avoid any structural issues or unexpected sagging over time, what specific safety factors or load distributions should i be looking at for the joists?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When considering the joists for your cedar deck with heavy railing, you should determine the maximum expected dead load which includes decking material, deck construction, railing, and any fixed items like planters. Safety factors, typically ranging from 1.6 to 2, are then applied to secure a margin for unpredicted loads and ensure the joists will not fail structurally. In terms of load distribution, all major concentrated loads should be evenly spaced to avoid undue stress on any single joist. Consider also bending stresses and deflection limits for your decking, which you'll commonly use as L/360 or L/240 where 'L' is the span length, adjusting your joist size and spacing accordingly. If you apply a safety factor and properly distribute loads, it should prevent sagging over time and maintain deck integrity.",
